<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!-- Generated by Pandoc using pretext.lua -->
<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="sec-solutions3">
    <title>Measuring concentration with percent composition</title>

    <p>
        When dealing with solutions, it is often very important to know how much
        solute is dissolved into a certain amount of volume of the solution.
        This quantity is called the <term><xref ref="def-concentration" text="custom">concentration</xref></term> of the solution.
        We have several different ways of expressing concentration.
        The first way that we’ll learn about is percent composition.
        There are three different forms of percent composition, mass-mass
        percent composition (% m/m), mass- volume percent composition (% m/v),
        and volume-volume percent composition (% v/v).
        The formulas for each is shown below: </p>
        <p>
        <me>\text{mass-mass percent}=\frac{\text{g solute}}{\text{g solution}}\times\ 100\%</me>
        <me>\text{mass-volume percent}=\frac{\text{g solute}}{\text{mL solution}}\times\ 100\%</me>
        <me>\text{volume-volume percent}=\frac{\text{mL solute}}{\text{mL solution}}\times\ 100\%</me>
    </p>

    <p>
        When calculating a percent composition, we simply have to use the
        formulas above, but we must make sure that the units that we're using
        match the units in the formula...if they don't we need to do some unit
        conversions first.
    </p>

    <p>
        We can also use percent composition to figure out how much solute is
        dissolved in a certain volume (or mass) of solution.  To do this, we must turn the percent composition into a conversion factor. See the example below:
    </p>
<assemblage>
    <title>Example problem showing how to use a percent composition as a conversion factor</title>
    <p>
        <term> Problem:  </term>How many grams of mL of ethanol are in a <m>250\ \text{mL}</m> bottle that contains a
        <m>65\%\ \text{v/v}</m> solution?
    </p>

    <p>
        <term>Solution:</term>
        </p>
<ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Create a conversion factor using the percent composition.
                </p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Since the values are percents, the bottom will always have a value of
        100
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>65\%\ \text{v/v}</m> ethanol= <m>\frac{65\ \text{mL ethanol}}{100\ \text{mL solution}}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </li>
            <li><p>Use dimensional analysis
                </p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>250\ \text{mL solution}\cdot\frac{65\ \text{mL ethanol}}{100\ \text{mL solution}}=162.5\ \text{mL ethanol}\xrightarrow{\text{Sig. Figs.}} 160\ \text{mL ethanol}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
                </li>
        </ol>
</assemblage>
    
    <exercises>
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercisegroup>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Calculate the mass-mass percent for the following solutions
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>3\ \text{g}</m> <m>NaCl</m> dissolved in <m>55\ \text{g}</m> solution
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>5\%\ \text{m/m}</m>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>3\ \text{g}</m> <m>NaCl</m> dissolved in <m>44\ \text{g}</m> of water (note that this is
                        NOT the grams of solution!)
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>6\%\ \text{m/m}</m>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>0.30\ \text{kg}</m> of ethanol dissolved in <m>4300\ \text{g}</m> of solution
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>7.0\%\ \text{m/m}</m>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercisegroup>
        <exercisegroup>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Calculate the mass-volume percent for the following
                    solutions
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>3\ \text{g}</m> <m>NaCl</m> dissolved in <m>45\ \text{mL}</m> of solution
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>7\%\ \text{m/v}</m>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>175\ \text{mg}</m> <m>NaCl</m> dissolved in <m>5.0\ \text{mL}</m> of solution
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>3.5\%\ \text{m/v}</m>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercisegroup>
        <exercisegroup>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Calculate the volume-volume percent for the following
                    solutions
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>32\ \text{mL}</m> of ethanol dissolved in <m>100\ \text{mL}</m> of solution
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>32\%\ \text{v/v}</m>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>165\ \text{μL}</m> of ethanol dissolved in <m>2.50\ \text{mL}</m> of solution
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <answer>
                    <p>
                        <m>6.60\%\ \text{v/v}</m>
                    </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercisegroup>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many grams of <m>NaCl</m> are in <m>35\ \text{mL}</m> of a <m>1.0\%\ \text{m/v}</m> solution?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>0.35\ \text{g}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many moles of <m>NaCl</m> are in <m>1.0\ \text{L}</m> of a <m>2.0\%\ \text{m/v}</m> solution?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <hint>
                <p>
                    Use the mass-volume percentage to find the number of grams of <m>NaCl</m>.  Then, how do we go from grams to moles?
                </p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>0.34\ \text{moles}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</section>